master is RED: B160's universal ampersand rule is falsified by B145's corpus, which the same batch merged - the engine writes to 'int3'. with no suffix, and our renderer now emits to 'int3&'.

MASTER DOES NOT PASS ITS OWN GATE. Verified by the coordinator on da0f4f5:

  test the_engine_suffixes_every_measured_conversion_target ... FAILED
  tests/b155_engine_wording_divergence.rs:157
  some measured engine messages do NOT suffix the target:
    ["Can't implicitly convert from 'const nat3' to 'int3'."]

  test result: FAILED. 5 passed; 1 failed
  Full log: x-alpha ~/.remote-builds/lsp-openplanet-bf0363fb/target/
            test-suite-logs/20260831T191201-suite.log

WHAT HAPPENED, and note that NEITHER MERGE WAS WRONG ON ITS OWN.

B160 adopted the engine spelling unconditionally -- `to '{to}&'.` -- on a
measurement of 22 of 22 messages carrying both marks. That population came from
exactly two corpora: b071 (enum targets) and b144 (funcdef-handle targets).

`corpus_files()` in b155_engine_wording_divergence.rs GLOBS EVERY .tsv IN
docs/probes/, so it auto-ingests any corpus added later. The very next merge
(B145, e28cdaf) added a third: b145-issue-repro-verdicts.tsv, 63 fixtures of
live engine verdicts measured against 1.29.14 on 2026-09-01.

Row 70, engine column, fixture 49-nat3-int3-conversion:

  Main.as(9,17): Can't implicitly convert from 'const nat3' to 'int3'.
  Main.as(12,17): No matching signatures to 'int3(const nat3&)'

Full stop: YES. Ampersand: NO. The 22/22 uniformity was TRUE OF ITS SAMPLE and
FALSE IN GENERAL -- both source corpora happened to carry reference targets.

AND THE SAME ROW CARRIES THE DISCRIMINATOR. The second message writes
`int3(const nat3&)` WITH an ampersand, because that parameter genuinely IS a
reference. So the engine is not decorating conversion targets at all; it prints
`&` when the type it is naming is a reference. Our renderer now appends one
unconditionally.

USER-VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE: for a value-type target we emit
`to 'int3&'.` where the engine says `to 'int3'.` The CLI footer asks users to
report differences from Openplanet's output, so this is a wrong message in the
one place we have made an explicit commitment to exactness.

THE FULL-STOP HALF OF B160 IS CORRECT AND SHOULD STAY. The engine message above
ends with `.`, as do all 22. Only the ampersand half is refuted.

THREE OPTIONS, none of them "pick an unconditional spelling":
  A. Revert the `&`, keep the full stop. Correct for int3, WRONG for the 22
     measured reference targets. Trades one wrong population for a larger one.
  B. Make the `&` conditional on the target actually being a reference binding.
     Correct, and the real work: it needs the target's binding kind at the
     diagnostic site, which may not be available there today. Establish that
     before promising it.
  C. Re-measure first. The b145 corpus now gives a third population and the
     discriminator is visible in a single row; derive the engine's actual rule
     from all three corpora before writing any renderer change.

RECOMMEND C THEN B. Do not choose an unconditional spelling to get master green
-- an unconditional rule is what produced this.

DO NOT "FIX" THIS BY NARROWING corpus_files() TO THE TWO ORIGINAL CORPORA. The
glob is why the counterexample was found at all, and the test did exactly the job
it was written for: it pinned a uniformity claim and fired the moment the claim
stopped holding. Narrowing it would restore the green and delete the finding.

This is also the second time in this batch that a 22/22-style uniformity has been
treated as "no per-case judgement required". See docs/verification-hazards.md,
"A detector that cannot see the counterexample gives a confident wrong answer",
and the entry on predicting the count before the run -- a sample can be uniform
because the sampling frame was uniform.

Related: B160 (the decision), B155 (the pin that caught it), B145 (the corpus
that refuted it), B165 (merged alongside B160).

MEASURED 2026-09-01 against live Openplanet 1.29.14, 16 variants, both controls
green, B077 sweep clean. Generator: scripts/probe_conversion_target_spelling.py.

BOTH CANDIDATE RULES ARE REFUTED. The ticket offered H-TYPE (the & tracks the
target type's category) and H-SLOT (it tracks whether the slot is a reference).
Neither survives:

  AMP+DOT  r1   enum L1 -> L2, var init        'L1' -> 'L2&'.
  AMP+DOT  r2   nat3 -> int3, var init         'nat3&' -> 'int3&'.
  AMP+DOT  r10  quat -> vec3, var init         'quat&' -> 'vec3&'.
  AMP+DOT  r11  vec3 -> int3, var init         'vec3&' -> 'int3&'.
  AMP+DOT  r12  A@ -> B@, var init             'A@&' -> 'B@&'.
  DOT      r6   nat3 -> int3, RETURN slot      'nat3&' -> 'int3'.
  DOT      r8   class -> int, var init         'C&' -> 'int'.
  DOT      r9   string -> int, var init        'string' -> 'int'.
  DOT      r13  CONST nat3 -> int3, var init   'const nat3' -> 'int3'.
  DOT      r14  const nat3& -> int3, var init  'const nat3&' -> 'int3'.

r2 AND r13 ARE THE SAME CONVERSION INTO THE SAME TARGET TYPE IN THE SAME
SYNTACTIC SLOT, and they disagree. The only variable is the SOURCE's constness.
H-TYPE cannot explain that pair; H-SLOT cannot explain r6 (a non-const reference
source whose target is unsuffixed because the slot is a return).

r13 reproduces B145's refuting row EXACTLY -- `'const nat3' to 'int3'.` -- from
a plain const local, so the original was not an artefact of CGameCtnChallenge
.Size being a property.

THE RULE THAT FITS ALL TEN. The target carries `&` iff ALL THREE hold:
  1. the SOURCE is non-const            (r13, r14 fail this)
  2. the TARGET type is not a primitive (r8, r9 fail this; enum counts as
                                         non-primitive here, per r1)
  3. the slot is an assignable variable, not a return (r6 fails this)

r3/r4/r5/r7 produced `No matching signatures` or the &inout diagnostic rather
than a conversion message, so they constrain nothing about spelling and are
recorded as n/a rather than scored as unsuffixed.

WHY THIS SETTLES THE PRODUCT QUESTION. Two of those three conditions -- source
constness and slot kind -- are not available at our diagnostic site, and
reproducing them means modelling AngelScript's value categories and const
propagation. That is a large change which would still be wrong wherever the
model is wrong, and being wrong in a NEW way is not an improvement on being
wrong in a uniform one.

AND THE UNSUFFIXED FORM IS THE COMMON REAL-WORLD CASE, not the exotic one:
condition 1 excludes every conversion from a const source, which is what every
engine property read is. B145's row is `int3 size = map.Size;` -- the shape a
plugin author actually writes.

DECISION: revert the `&`, KEEP the full stop. `Can't implicitly convert from
'{from}' to '{to}'.` That is byte-exact with the engine for const sources,
return slots and primitive targets, and differs by one character in the
non-const-source object-target case. B160's full-stop half was never in doubt:
all 10 measured messages end with `.`, as did all 22 before them.

This is a narrowing of B160, not a revert of it. Recorded here rather than
reopening B160, which is closed and whose measurement was correct for its
sample.